\paragraph{Why the overcharged static example is insufficient.} In the spherical charged lapse \(f(r)=1-2m/r+q^2/r^2\), assume \(q^2>m^2\) and \(q\ne0\). Since \(r^2f(r)=(r-m)^2+q^2-m^2>0\), there is no positive horizon root. Outgoing radial null coordinate time satisfies \(dt/dr=f^{-1}\), and near zero \(f^{-1}\sim r^2/q^2\), which is integrable. Thus the punctured static geometry permits local outgoing visibility from arbitrarily near its singular center.
\paragraph{Regular-center countercheck.} Its static spatial metric has radial proper length element \(dr/\sqrt f\sim r\,dr/|q|\), so the puncture is at finite proper distance. In addition the nonzero constant flux charge gives an electric field of order \(q/r^2\), incompatible with a smooth regular center. Consequently this static family cannot be substituted for a spacetime dynamically formed from the source's regular charged-collapse data.
\paragraph{Remaining target.} Even an admissible isolated exceptional collapse example would not settle a conjecture about generic spacetimes. The first gap is dynamical control of area radius, mass and charge near the terminal endpoint \(\widehat\Gamma\), together with stability or genericity in the source data class. This attempt only rejects an attractive but inadmissible static shortcut. It proves neither local censorship nor a counterexample to the generic charged spherical statement and does not replace it by the uncharged problem.

